60536.sol:
Let $y=\frac{1-x+x^{2}}{1+x+x^{2}}=1-\frac{2 x}{1+x+x^{2}} \begin{aligned}&=1-\frac{2}{\frac{1}{x}+1+x}\\
\Rightarrow \quad y &=1-\frac{2}{t}
\end{aligned}$ where t=$\frac{1}{x}+1+x Now, $ y$ is minimum, when \frac{2}{t}$ is $\max \Rightarrow t$ is $\mathrm\min$. $\begin{array}{lc}
\therefore& \frac{d t}{d x}=-\frac{1}{x^{2}}+1=0 \\
\Rightarrow \quad x=\pm1 \\
\frac{d^{2} t}{d x^{2}}=\frac{2}{x^{3}}>0, \text{ for } x=1
\end{array}  \therefore$ Minimum value of $y$ is  \begin{aligned}1-$\frac{2}{1+1+1}$&=1-$\frac{2}{3}$\\
&=$\frac{1}{3}$
\end{aligned}

